WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:02.550
To date, a case has not been documented

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.620
in which the coronavirus
has been transmitted

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
to humans from surfaces
contaminated with the virus.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.190
Still, the Centers for
Disease Control and Prevention

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.260
recommends that frequently
touched surfaces

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.180
in your house should be
routinely cleaned and disinfected

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.540
to lessen the chances of transmission.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.440
But how exactly should you disinfect

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.207
all the surfaces in your home?

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.977
First off, be sure to read
the labels of your products

00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:32.780
to ensure correct usage and safety.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.060
Use EPA-approved disinfectants,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.020
and it's best to use disposable gloves.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.498
If using reusable gloves,
be sure to use them

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:43.506
only while cleaning and
disinfecting for the coronavirus.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.550
Always wash your hands immediately after

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:47.996
taking the gloves off.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.330
Focus on cleaning and disinfecting

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.050
the most frequently touched
surfaces in your home.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.790
Think tables, doorknobs, handles,

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.292
light switches, desks, toilets,

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:02.501
faucets, sinks, and electronics.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.400
When it comes to electronics,
research suggests

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.330
that the virus can last a long time

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.860
on these types of surfaces.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.770
For nonporous surfaces,
like a keyboard or a phone,

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.950
research from the National
Institutes of Health,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.910
CDC, Princeton University, and UCLA

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.600
says that the virus could live on them

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.690
for two to three days.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.300
The CDC suggests removing

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.920
any visible contaminant if possible.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.190
To disinfect, you should
follow the instructions

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.570
from the manufacturer.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.720
Apple recently updated its guidelines

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.450
for disinfecting its products.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.060
To clean your iPhone, you can clean it

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.560
with a damp lens cloth
and then gently wipe it

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.250
with a Clorox disinfecting wipe

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.750
while avoiding getting any moisture

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.191
in any of the openings.

00:01:45.191 --> 00:01:46.720
You can also use the wipes on your other

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.570
Apple products, such as your laptop.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.090
If your electronics
manufacturer doesn't provide

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.680
disinfecting instructions,
the CDC suggests

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.110
using alcohol wipes or
sprays with at least

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.380
70% alcohol for touch screens.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.360
And all surfaces should
be thoroughly dried

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.726
to avoid any pools of liquid forming.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.380
For hard surfaces, the coronavirus appears

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.350
to last the longest on
plastic and stainless steel,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.803
about two to three days.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.740
If your surfaces are dirty, be sure to

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.320
first clean them with a detergent

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.573
or soap and water before you disinfect.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.950
You can then use an EPA-approved

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.783
disinfectant on the surface.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.940
Diluted household bleach
solutions are an option,

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.550
but make sure to follow the
manufacturer's instructions

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.990
for application. Make
sure it's not expired,

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.410
and never mix household
bleach with ammonia

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.020
or any other cleanser.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.550
Make sure to open a window so you get

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.823
plenty of ventilation while
and after applying the bleach.

00:02:44.823 --> 00:02:46.533
To make a bleach solution,

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.680
mix 5 tablespoons of
bleach per gallon of water

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.672
or 4 teaspoons of bleach
per quart of water.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.720
For soft surfaces, such as carpeted floor,

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.420
rugs, and drapes, first wipe off any dirt

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.000
or contaminants you can see,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.100
then use an approved
cleaner while following

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.460
the cleaner's manufacturer instructions.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.500
If possible, put the items in the laundry

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.450
according to their
manufacturer instructions,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.652
using the warmest
appropriate water setting.

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:16.319
Be sure to dry thoroughly.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.330
Dirty laundry from an ill person

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.750
can be washed with other people's items.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.160
But if you need to handle laundry

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.890
for someone who's sick,
use disposable gloves

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.670
or gloves that are dedicated to cleaning

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.000
and disinfecting for the coronavirus.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.760
Do not shake dirty laundry.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.320
This will minimize the possibility

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.330
of dispersing the virus through the air.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.900
Use the warmest water setting
appropriate for your items

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.520
and make sure they're dried thoroughly.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.380
Also, be sure to clean
and disinfect your hamper.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.210
If possible, you should use liners

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.270
that are disposable or washable.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.800
And, finally, clean your hands

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.220
immediately after using the gloves.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.277
If you don't use gloves,

00:03:55.277 --> 00:03:56.607
be sure to just wash your hands

00:03:56.607 --> 00:03:59.110
immediately after handling the laundry.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.340
Make sure you follow the CDC's additional

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.170
home-care guidelines on its website

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.890
when caring for a sick person.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.810
If possible, allow a person to clean

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.910
and disinfect their own
bedroom and bathroom.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.080
And just a reminder: Cleaning surfaces

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.400
is not a substitute for washing hands.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.778
Experts say washing your
hands with soap and water

00:04:16.778 --> 00:04:18.280
and avoiding touching your face

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.270
are still the best ways to
avoid contracting the virus.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.303
So, at the very least,
keep washing those hands.

